{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Both roadmaps updated and committed, and the probe is preserved as a reusable tool. Where things landed:\n\n**The probe settled the biggest unknown.** A genuine `encrypted_content` blob from your July 18 session, spliced into a brand-new fabricated rollout, resumed cleanly under a different CLI version with zero API errors and normal behavior — and the ~6k token jump versus the no-injection control suggests the blob was genuinely transmitted and processed. So the encryption is provably *not* keyed to the session or conversation. Your two hypotheses are exactly what remains: one global key versus per-account keying. Those are indistinguishable from a single account — the moment you have access to a second OpenAI account, `node scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs <donor-rollout>` run under it answers the question in one shot.\n\n**One correction to the scenario framing:** same-machine `codex → turnbridge → codex` currently doesn't lose anything even for habitual turnbridge users, because turnbridge routes same-CLI resumes through native `codex resume` — the original rollout, reasoning included, is what gets loaded. The loss cases are: different machine, deleted rollout, and the sneaky one — a `codex → claude → codex` round trip, where the return leg is a fabrication from ledger events even though source and target are both Codex.\n\n**What's recorded where:**\n- cledger's README roadmap now has \"Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state\" — the record-everything-JIC argument, the proposed shape (raw-only tier like the 0.5.0 unrecognized-line preservation: out of transcripts, titles, and secret scans — which can't scan ciphertext anyway), consumer opt-in restore, and the open questions (cross-account, TTL, ~2–4KB/turn size cost).\n- turnbridge's design-doc roadmap has the consumer side: replay only when source provider/CLI matches the target (Codex blobs never go to Claude Code, per your rule), the probe result, and the cross-account caveat.\n\nThe natural sequence when you decide to build it: cledger grows the opaque tier first, then turnbridge's codex fabricator threads matching blobs back in — with the probe as the regression test for both.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"11ecb696686cd6f4e88035fcf93f30a8e5c29bf0","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","seq":597},"id":"ev1-7aa0a175b72af5579f4f98fca2b28427c683fb3c0b7ec3b4976973c14075713b","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-22T00:06:30.110Z","producer":{"session_id":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.9.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Both roadmaps updated and committed, and the probe is preserved as a reusable tool. Where things landed:\n\n**The probe settled the biggest unknown.** A genuine `encrypted_content` blob from your July 18 session, spliced into a brand-new fabricated rollout, resumed cleanly under a different CLI version with zero API errors and normal behavior — and the ~6k token jump versus the no-injection control suggests the blob was genuinely transmitted and processed. So the encryption is provably *not* keyed to the session or conversation. Your two hypotheses are exactly what remains: one global key versus per-account keying. Those are indistinguishable from a single account — the moment you have access to a second OpenAI account, `node scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs <donor-rollout>` run under it answers the question in one shot.\n\n**One correction to the scenario framing:** same-machine `codex → turnbridge → codex` currently doesn't lose anything even for habitual tur